---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import ContentNote from '../components/content/ContentNote.astro';
import EvidenceTable from '../components/content/EvidenceTable.astro';
import RelatedReading from '../components/content/RelatedReading.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Cooked Score Ranges Explained | Cooked Finance"
  description="A supportive guide to Cooked Score ranges from 0 to 100, including what each range may suggest and the most useful next step."
  path="/score-ranges"
  eyebrow="Score guide"
  heading="Your range is a starting point, not a label."
  lead="Use the total to orient yourself, then use the four components to decide what deserves attention."
  updated="August 17, 2026"
>
  <ContentNote title="Read the components, not just the total">
    <p>
      Two people can receive the same total for very different reasons. A thin
      cash buffer calls for a different response than heavy monthly debt
      payments. The component breakdown is more actionable than the headline.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <EvidenceTable caption="Cooked Score 2.0.0 interpretation ranges">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Supportive label</th><th scope="col">What the model may be seeing</th><th scope="col">Useful first move</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">0–24</th><td>Needs Attention</td><td>A few foundations need attention. Start with one manageable move.</td><td>Protect essentials, avoid missed payments, and make a short cash-flow plan.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">25–44</th><td>Running Hot</td><td>Some pressure is showing, but the clearest improvements are within reach.</td><td>Strengthen the weakest useful component with one repeatable monthly action.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">45–64</th><td>Finding Balance</td><td>The foundation is taking shape, with room to strengthen a few pillars.</td><td>Preserve what works and focus on the clearest remaining constraint.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">65–84</th><td>Steady Heat</td><td>Several healthy foundations are working together. Keep the rhythm going.</td><td>Review the weaker components and the important risks outside this model.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">85–100</th><td>Cooking Confidently</td><td>The entered figures show strong foundations across most or all pillars.</td><td>Do not chase a perfect score; use a complete plan for priorities the model cannot see.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </EvidenceTable>

  <h2>If your result is lower than expected</h2>
  <p>
    Start by checking scope and units. Combining your income with a partner’s
    expenses, entering an annual debt total instead of monthly payments, or
    including retirement money as liquid cash can distort the result. Then ask
    whether the weakest component reflects a temporary event or an ongoing pattern.
  </p>
  <p>
    A low score is not evidence of irresponsibility. Medical costs, job loss,
    education, divorce, caregiving, discrimination, and local housing costs can
    all shape the inputs. If essentials or required payments are currently at
    risk, immediate stability matters more than improving a calculator result.
  </p>

  <h2>If your result is higher than expected</h2>
  <p>
    A high score does not confirm that you have suitable insurance, an estate
    plan, a diversified portfolio, appropriate tax choices, safe credit habits,
    or enough retirement income. It also does not test whether savings are
    earmarked for near-term spending. Use the result as permission to ask better
    questions—not to stop reviewing your finances.
  </p>

  <h2>What a score change means</h2>
  <p>
    Compare version 2.0.0 results only with other version 2.0.0 results using the
    the same person’s figures. A one- or two-point move may be rounding or normal
    monthly variation. A sustained component change is usually more meaningful
    than a single total-score change.
  </p>

  <ContentNote title="No lender or regulator uses these ranges" tone="caution">
    <p>
      These labels belong only to Cooked Finance. They are not credit tiers,
      underwriting categories, regulated definitions, or professional ratings.
    </p>
  </ContentNote>

  <RelatedReading links={[
    { href: '/financial-health-score/', label: 'What the score measures', description: 'Learn the purpose and limits of the seven-input snapshot.' },
    { href: '/guides/what-to-do-after-a-low-score/', label: 'After a low score', description: 'Work through a calm, practical sequence without shame.' },
    { href: '/methodology/', label: 'Check the formula', description: 'Review all weights, anchors, and model assumptions.' },
  ]} />
</PageLayout>
